      <h2>The Tormek Way: How the System Works</h2>
      <p>The T-8 replaces the bench grinder, the coarse stone and the fine stone with one wheel and one strop. Everything happens at low speed in water, so the edge never gets hot enough to lose its temper, no sparks fly, and the swarf ends up in the trough instead of in the air. The important ideas:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>One stone, two grits.</strong> The SG-250 is a fast-cutting 220 grit stone. Rubbing the fine side of the SP-650 Stone Grader on it crushes the surface grains so it behaves like a 1000 grit stone. You shape the bevel on the coarse surface, grade the stone fine, and finish the bevel <em>without touching the jig setting</em>, so the fine scratches land exactly on the coarse ones.</li>
        <li><strong>The universal support is the reference.</strong> Every jig rides on the 12 mm universal support bar. Its height (set by the Micro Adjust) and position (vertical sleeves or horizontal base) determine the edge angle. The TT-50 truing tool also rides on the bar, which guarantees the stone surface is parallel to the bar and therefore to the tool.</li>
        <li><strong>Repeatability.</strong> Because the angle is set mechanically, the second sharpening removes almost no steel: the bevel is already the right shape and you only refresh it. Tools last decades.</li>
        <li><strong>Hone away the burr.</strong> Grinding raises a burr. The leather wheel with PA-70 compound removes it gently and polishes both faces to a mirror. A honed edge is sharper and stays sharp longer.</li>
      </ul>
