      <h2>Setup &amp; First Use</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Unpack and inspect.</strong> Check for transit damage and report it to the dealer right away. The stone ships mounted; check that the EzyLock is snug (turn the stone anticlockwise by hand until it stops).</li>
        <li><strong>Bench height.</strong> Put the machine on a stable surface. For knife work Tormek recommends a bench of 750&ndash;830 mm (29-1/2&ndash;32-2/3&quot;); for chisels and plane irons a normal workbench is fine. Sitting works well for scissors and small tools. The rubber feet keep it put; it does not need bolting down, and the downward load on the vertical universal support steadies it further.</li>
        <li><strong>Light.</strong> Good light on the stone matters more than on any other machine because you steer by watching the water and the scratch pattern. A lamp above and slightly in front of the stone is ideal.</li>
        <li><strong>Register the machine</strong> at tormek.com to extend the warranty from 5 to 8 years.</li>
        <li><strong>Prepare the leather wheel</strong> with oil and compound before the first honing (see <em>Honing Wheels</em>).</li>
        <li><strong>Fill the trough and run in the stone</strong> as described next.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="video">
        <div class="frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/5d8jcG6bzJ4" title="Tormek T-8 sharpening system: Getting Started with Alan Holtham" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
        <p class="caption">Tormek: <em>Tormek T-8 sharpening system: Getting Started with Alan Holtham</em> (10:48)</p>
      </div>

      <h3>Filling the Water Trough</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Raise the trough with the screw-lift knob until it sits under the stone.</li>
        <li>Start the machine and pour water in until it reaches the max line inside the trough.</li>
        <li>Keep pouring. A dry stone soaks up about 1.2 litres (2 pints) before the level stays put. Top up until the water stops disappearing.</li>
        <li>The stone must run in water the whole time you grind. If the water stops flowing over the top of the stone, add more.</li>
        <li>For long blades clip the extended water cover onto the tab on the trough lip so the run-off goes back into the trough instead of down the housing.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="note">
        Only plain water. No soap, antifreeze or rust inhibitor is needed; the stone sheds steel particles into the trough and the magnet catches them.
      </div>

      <h3>Running In a New Stone</h3>
      <p>A new SG-250 is ready to use as soon as it is saturated, but it pays to spend five minutes on it first:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>With the stone running wet, mount the universal support vertically and lower it with the Micro Adjust until the bar <em>almost</em> touches the stone. Turn the stone slowly by hand and watch the gap. If it opens and closes, the stone is slightly out of round, which is normal for a new stone that has been shipped.</li>
        <li>True it with the TT-50 at a shallow cut (0.25 mm) until the diamond touches all the way around. This gives you a surface that is both round and parallel to the universal support.</li>
        <li>Rub the stone briefly with the coarse side of the SP-650 to expose fresh grit; a freshly trued surface can feel glazed.</li>
        <li>Read the new diameter off the label scale (it will now be a little under 250 mm) and use that number on the WM-200 from now on.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Water Level and After Use</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Expect to top up the trough as you work; a saturated stone still carries water out over the rim. A spray of water across the top of the stone means it is wet enough.</li>
        <li>When you finish, lower the trough so the stone is not left standing in water. A stone that sits with one segment submerged for days absorbs water unevenly, runs out of balance until it dries, and the soaked segment wears faster.</li>
        <li>Empty and rinse the trough often. Pull the magnetic scraper, let it release the swarf into a bin, and wipe out the sludge. Do not pour it down a sink; it sets like cement in the trap.</li>
      </ul>
