      <h2>The Universal Support</h2>
      <p>Where you put the bar decides which way the stone runs relative to the edge, and that decides how the tool behaves.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Stone runs</th><th>Use it for</th><th>Character</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Vertical</strong> (sleeves above the stone)</td><td>Toward the edge (edge-leading)</td><td>SE-77 chisels and plane irons, TT-50 truing, most jigs, knife jigs on the T-8</td><td>Fastest steel removal. Water flows over the edge so you see the burr form less clearly. The wheel pulls the tool into the support, which is stable. Never free-hand in this direction with the bar as a rest.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Horizontal</strong> (base on the stone side)</td><td>Away from the edge (edge-trailing)</td><td>Free-hand grinding, bowl gouges in the SVD-186 R, roughing gouges and skews in the SVS-50, thin knives, axes and adzes on the bar</td><td>No risk of the tool digging into the stone. You can watch the burr develop. Slightly slower and leaves a slightly finer surface.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Horizontal, honing side</strong> (base beside the leather wheel)</td><td>Away from the edge</td><td>Honing with the tool still in its jig (SE-77, SVD-186 R, knife jigs)</td><td>The only direction allowed on leather. Set the WM-200 to 220 mm, not the stone diameter.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p01_02.jpg" alt="Two pictograms of the T-8 viewed from above: on the left the universal support is vertical over the grindstone for grinding toward the edge; on the right it is in the horizontal base beside the honing wheel for honing away from the edge" />
        <figcaption>Positioning the machine for the SE-77: grinding toward the edge on the stone (left), honing away from the edge on the leather wheel (right)</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Micro Adjust</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Turn the scaled nut to raise or lower the bar. One graduation moves the bar 0.25 mm, which changes a 25&deg; chisel bevel by a fraction of a degree, so it is fine enough to split the difference between the WM-200 setting and the marker method.</li>
        <li>Always finish by pressing the bar down so the nut sits firmly on its sleeve, then tighten <strong>both</strong> locking knobs. A bar that is clamped with the nut floating will settle during grinding and change the angle.</li>
        <li>Loosening the knobs, raising the bar out of the way and dropping it back on the same nut setting reproduces the angle exactly. This is what makes the grade-fine-then-finish sequence work.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Replicating an Existing Angle: The Marker Method</h3>
      <p>Whenever a tool already has the bevel you want, skip the AngleMaster and copy the bevel instead. Colour the whole bevel with a permanent marker, mount the tool in its jig, bring it to the stone and turn the stone backwards by hand a few times. Look at where the ink has been rubbed off: only at the tip means the angle is too steep, only at the heel means it is too shallow. Nudge the Micro Adjust until the ink disappears evenly from tip to heel. Now grind. This is the fastest, most accurate way to re-sharpen anything you have ground before.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p04_02.jpg" alt="Three drawings: colouring a chisel bevel with a marker, turning the grindstone by hand with the chisel in the SE-77 to see where the ink is removed, and adjusting the Micro Adjust" />
        <figcaption>The marker method: ink the bevel, rotate the stone by hand, adjust until the wheel wipes the ink from heel to tip</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="video">
        <div class="frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/VEgMd-SRYbs" title="The Tormek Marker Method" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
        <p class="caption">Tormek: <em>The Tormek Marker Method</em> (0:43)</p>
