      <h3>The Bar as a Tool Rest and Free-hand Grinding</h3>
      <p>With the bar horizontal and no more than 2 mm from the stone, it is a tool rest. Rest your hands or the tool on it and grind edge-trailing. Very short carving chisels rest directly on the bar with the shoulder as a stop; axes and adzes rest on the bar with your index finger as a stop; pocket and carving knives are held with the fingers close to the edge for control. Keep your elbows in, use light pressure on thin blades so they do not flex, and grade the stone fine first for knives. Free-hand always means edge-trailing: the only reason the Tormek allows grinding toward the edge is that a jig stops the tool from being dragged into the stone.</p>

      <h2>SG-250 Grindstone &amp; SP-650 Stone Grader</h2>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/sp650_p01_01.jpg" alt="Photo of the SP-650 Stone Grader being pressed against the grindstone of a T-8 with both hands, with the manufacturer's bullet points beside it" />
        <figcaption>SP-650 Stone Grader: silicon carbide block with a fine face and a coarse face</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>How Grading Works</h3>
      <p>A soft, fast-cutting stone and a hard, fine stone are opposite things, and no single stone can be both. The SG-250 is built to be fast (220 grit, loosely bonded so worn grains fall away and fresh ones take over). The stone grader works around the compromise by changing the <em>surface</em> of the stone rather than the stone itself:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fine side</strong> (smooth face): pressed hard onto the running stone, it crushes the exposed grain tips and burnishes the surface so it cuts like a 1000 grit stone. Steel removal drops, surface finish improves. The effect wears off as you grind through the crushed layer.</li>
        <li><strong>Coarse side</strong> (rough face): pressed onto the stone and moved sideways, it tears the burnished layer off and exposes fresh sharp grain. This returns the stone to 220 grit and also <em>reactivates</em> a glazed stone that has stopped cutting (common after grinding large HSS surfaces such as planer blades).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Neither operation wears the stone appreciably and both can be repeated as often as you like. Do not use the SP-650 on a diamond wheel; on a Japanese waterstone only the fine side is used, for cleaning.</p>

      <h3>When to Grade Coarse vs. Fine</h3>
      <table class="settings-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Stone surface</th><th>How</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Re-shaping a bevel, changing the angle, grinding out a nick or chip, flattening a back</td><td>Coarse (220)</td><td>Coarse side, high pressure, 20&ndash;30 s, moving sideways across the stone</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Finishing a bevel after shaping (same jig setting)</td><td>Fine (~1000)</td><td>Fine side, high pressure, 20&ndash;30 s (the SE-77 sheet says about 45 s for chisels and plane irons)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Touching up an edge that still has its shape (marker method set-up)</td><td>Fine from the start</td><td>Grade fine first, then grind lightly</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Thin knives, carving tools, anything you grind free-hand</td><td>Fine</td><td>A fine surface is gentler and less likely to grab a thin blade</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Stone has stopped cutting (glazed, shiny, slow)</td><td>Reactivate</td><td>Coarse side, high pressure, 20&ndash;30 s; use the corners of the grader for the best bite</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Stone is out of round or grooved</td><td>Not a grader job</td><td>True it with the TT-50; the grader cannot correct shape</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/sp650_p02_01.jpg" alt="Three drawings: a plane iron being ground in the SE-77, the fine side of the stone grader pressed on the stone, and the same plane iron ground again lightly on the graded stone" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/sp650_p02_02.jpg" alt="Drawing of the coarse side of the stone grader being moved sideways across the running stone" />
      </div>
      <h3>Technique</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Rest your wrists, not the grader, on the universal support. The silicon carbide block will wear a flat on the bar if you drag it across.</li>
        <li>Use real pressure; a timid pass does nothing. Keep the block moving side to side so the whole width of the stone is treated.</li>
        <li>After grading fine, grind with <em>light</em> pressure. Pressing hard cuts through the fine layer and brings back the coarse scratches.</li>
        <li>You can feel the difference: a fine-graded stone feels slick under a fingertip, a coarse one grabs.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="video">
        <div class="frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/eYNkEKS5-KY" title="Tormek Stone Grader SP-650" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
        <p class="caption">Tormek: <em>Tormek Stone Grader SP-650</em> (1:53)</p>
      </div>

      <h3>Keeping the Stone Round, Flat and Alive</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Spread the wear. When shaping a narrow tool, keep it moving across the stone so it does not dig a groove. A groove in the stone becomes a hump on the next wide tool.</li>
        <li>Check trueness every few sessions by lowering the bar to within a hair of the stone and turning it by hand. Any wobble: true it now. A small error grows because the tool dives into the low spot and wears it deeper. Frequent light truing costs far less stone than occasional heavy truing.</li>
        <li>The flat side of the stone is for flattening backs. Keep the water trough full so the side stays wet, and keep the universal support close by as a rest.</li>
        <li>A professional sharpener wore a stone from 250 to 200 mm after about 2,000 kitchen knives; in a home shop the stone lasts many years. Replace it below 180 mm.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>TT-50 Truing Tool</h2>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/tt50_p03_01.jpg" alt="Photo of the TT-50 truing tool mounted on the universal support over the grindstone, with a hand on each feed knob, and the manufacturer's bullet points" />
        <figcaption>TT-50 Truing Tool on the universal support</figcaption>
      </div>
      <p>The TT-50 is a diamond dresser that slides along the universal support and is fed across the stone by two knobs on a threaded rod. Because it references the same bar the jigs ride on, the surface it leaves is exactly round and exactly parallel to the bar. The cutter is a copper cylinder with diamond grains scattered through it; the copper wears away and leaves the diamonds standing proud, and new diamonds surface as old ones wear. The copper between the diamonds wears down immediately in the first use, which can make a new tip look damaged. It is not.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/tt50_p03_02.jpg" alt="Drawing of the TT-50 on the T-8 with callouts: 1 universal support, 2 feed knobs, 3 diamond cutting tip, 4 Micro Adjust scale" />
        <figcaption>1 universal support &middot; 2 feed knobs &middot; 3 diamond cutter &middot; 4 Micro Adjust scale</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>Truing Procedure</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Mount the universal support <strong>vertically</strong> (the TT-50 only works in this position) and set its height so the truing tool can slide on.</li>
        <li>Slide the TT-50 onto the bar. Push it firmly against the Micro Adjust leg (5) so it cannot twist, and lock it with its locking knob (6).</li>
        <li>Start the machine and fill the trough.</li>
        <li>Lower the bar with the Micro Adjust (4) until the diamond just touches the <em>highest</em> spot on the stone; you will hear it tick once per revolution.</li>
        <li>Stop the machine. Wind the cutter sideways with one of the feed knobs until it is clear of the stone.</li>
        <li>Set the depth of cut (a) by turning the Micro Adjust: each graduation is 0.25 mm. One graduation is a normal pass; never more than three (0.75 mm).</li>
        <li>Press the bar down so the nut seats on its sleeve again and tighten both locking screws (7).</li>
        <li>Start the machine and feed the cutter across the stone by turning the two knobs alternately, hand over hand. Take about <strong>90 seconds</strong> to cross the full 50 mm width.</li>
        <li>Run the cutter back across at the same setting, or lower by another graduation and repeat, until the diamond cuts all the way round on every part of the width. Finish with a slow light pass.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/tt50_p04_01.jpg" alt="Nine-step picture sequence from the TT-50 instruction: setting the support height, locking the tool against the leg, filling the trough, lowering to touch the high spot, moving the cutter clear, setting 0.25 mm depth, locking the support, feeding across in 90 seconds, and the 30-second fast feed option" />
        <figcaption>The TT-50 truing sequence</figcaption>
      </div>
      <table class="settings-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Universal support position</td><td>Vertical</td><td>Required; the TT-50 cannot be used in the horizontal base</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Depth per pass</td><td>0.25&ndash;0.75 mm (0.01&ndash;0.03&quot;)</td><td>1&ndash;3 graduations on the Micro Adjust; use 0.25 mm for routine truing</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Feed rate, normal</td><td>~90 s across the stone</td><td>Leaves a smooth surface. Feeding too slowly causes chatter and vibration</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Feed rate, aggressive surface</td><td>~30 s across, max 0.25 mm depth</td><td>Leaves a rougher surface that removes steel faster, for heavy re-shaping</td></tr>
          <tr><td>How often</td><td>As soon as you notice any unevenness</td><td>Also before any precision job such as a wide plane iron, and after grinding narrow tools in one spot</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="warning">
        Never use the TT-50 on a diamond wheel. Diamond wheels do not need truing and the dresser would ruin both.
      </div>
      <h3>Truing After Dressing, and Dressing After Truing</h3>
      <p>Truing and grading interact. A freshly trued stone has a slightly closed, burnished surface, so after truing give it a few seconds with the coarse side of the SP-650 before shaping work, or simply go straight to the fine side if you are about to finish an edge. Conversely, the stone grader cannot fix a stone that is out of round, so if the tool in the jig touches only part of the stone&rsquo;s width or ticks once per revolution, true first and grade second. Write the new diameter down (or just read it from the label each time) because every truing pass takes 0.25&ndash;0.75 mm off the diameter and the WM-200 setting must follow it.</p>
