      <h2>WM-200 AngleMaster</h2>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/wm200_p01_01.jpg" alt="Labelled drawing of the WM-200 AngleMaster: measuring grooves along the top edge from 15 to 75 degrees, the diameter compensator with its 150 to 250 mm scale and knob at lower left, the angle setter arm with its 10 to 75 degree scale at right, and the separate MB-100 scale" />
        <figcaption>WM-200: measuring grooves (top edge), diameter compensator (left knob), angle setter (right arm). The MB-100 scale is only for grinding on the side of a diamond wheel.</figcaption>
      </div>
      <p>The AngleMaster does two things: it sets a jig to grind any edge angle from 10&deg; to 75&deg; on any stone diameter from 250 mm down to 150 mm, and it measures the angle already on a tool. Two magnets on the back stick it to the metal plate on the T-8 housing so it is always at hand.</p>
      <h3>Zeroing to the Stone Diameter</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Read the stone diameter off the scale printed on the machine label next to the stone. There is no need to measure with a caliper.</li>
        <li>Loosen the knob on the <strong>diameter compensator</strong> (the curved foot at the lower left) and slide it until its index lines up with that diameter. The scale is in 5 mm steps from 250 down to 150 mm, with inch marks for 10&quot; to 6&quot;. Tighten the knob.</li>
        <li>Re-check this every time you true the stone. On a worn stone the same jig height gives a steeper angle, and the compensator is what keeps the reading honest.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/wm200_p03_02.jpg" alt="Two drawings: the diameter scale printed on the T-8 label beside the stone reading 230 mm, and the WM-200 diameter compensator set to 230 mm" />
        <figcaption>Read the diameter from the label scale and transfer it to the compensator</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>Setting an Edge Angle on a Jig-mounted Tool</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Mount the tool in its jig with the specified projection and set the jig on the universal support.</li>
        <li>Loosen the knob on the <strong>angle setter</strong> (the pivoting arm at the right) and set its pointer to the angle you want. The scale reads to 1&deg;. Tighten.</li>
        <li>Hold the AngleMaster so the diameter compensator foot sits on the stone and the flat face of the angle setter arm lies on the tool&rsquo;s <em>bevel</em>, with the whole base of the arm on the steel and the corner (a) of the compensator touching the stone.</li>
        <li>Raise or lower the universal support with the Micro Adjust until the arm sits flat on the bevel with no light under either end. Lock the support.</li>
        <li>Confirm with a marker if the tool already has a bevel; the two methods should agree within a graduation of the Micro Adjust.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/wm200_p02_01.jpg" alt="Drawing of the WM-200 setting a plane iron in the SE-77 to 25 degrees on a 250 mm grindstone" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/wm200_p02_02.jpg" alt="Drawing of the same 25 degree setting on a stone worn down to 180 mm, with the diameter compensator moved to 180" />
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p04_03.jpg" alt="Three drawings: the WM-200 diameter compensator being set, the AngleMaster held against a chisel in the SE-77 while the Micro Adjust is turned, and a close-up showing the whole base of the angle setter resting on the tool with its corner on the wheel" />
        <figcaption>Setting a new angle on the SE-77: set the diameter, then adjust the support height until the AngleMaster base lies flat on the bevel with corner (a) on the wheel</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="note">
        <strong>Honing with the jig:</strong> when you move the universal support to the leather wheel, set the compensator to <strong>220 mm</strong> (the diameter of the LA-220), not the stone diameter, then set the same angle again. Otherwise the honing angle will not match the grinding angle.
      </div>
      <h3>Measuring an Existing Angle</h3>
      <p>The notches along the top edge are gauges from 15&deg; to 75&deg;. Slip the edge of the tool into a notch; the one that fits snugly on both faces is the included angle. For single-bevel tools (chisels, plane irons) that is the edge angle. For knives, which have two bevels, the notch reads the total included angle, so a knife measuring 30&deg; has 15&deg; per side.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/wm200_p04_01.jpg" alt="Two drawings: a knife edge seated in the 25 degree measuring groove of the WM-200, and a bowl gouge seated in the 45 degree groove" />
        <figcaption>Measuring an existing edge angle with the grooves</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="video">
        <div class="frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/zFmGfDULpiQ" title="Tormek AngleMaster WM-200" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
        <p class="caption">Tormek: <em>Tormek AngleMaster WM-200</em> (1:48)</p>
      </div>

      <h2>SE-77 Square Edge Jig</h2>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p01_01.jpg" alt="Photo of a chisel clamped in the SE-77 Square Edge Jig being ground on the T-8, beside line drawings of a plane iron and two wood chisels with the limits max width 77 mm and max thickness 9 mm" />
        <figcaption>SE-77 for plane irons and wood chisels: max width 77 mm (3&quot;), max thickness 9 mm (3/8&quot;)</figcaption>
      </div>
      <p>The SE-77 clamps a chisel or plane iron square to the universal support and slides along it on nylon bushings. The edge angle comes from the height of the bar, set with the Micro Adjust. Two features set it apart from a generic honing guide: a shoulder on the upper base that registers the tool&rsquo;s flat side so it cannot be clamped twisted, and an <strong>adjustable side</strong> (two small screws, 5) that lets you tilt the clamp by a fraction of a degree to correct an edge that is coming out of square, or deliberately make it convex. The lower clamp has a central ridge so short, tapered tools such as Japanese chisels still clamp firmly, and the clamping force is spread to the ends so the knobs need no heroics.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p02_01.jpg" alt="Labelled drawing of the SE-77 on the T-8: 1 inner safety stop, 2 outer safety stop, 3 Micro Adjust, 4 universal support, 5 adjustment screws" />
        <figcaption>1 inner (movable) stop &middot; 2 outer stop &middot; 3 Micro Adjust &middot; 4 universal support &middot; 5 adjustment screws of the adjustable side</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p02_02.jpg" alt="Three detail drawings: the alignment shoulder A that squares the tool, the clamp ridge that spreads pressure to the edges for tapered chisels, and the two adjustment screws 5 of the adjustable side" />
        <figcaption>Shoulder (A) squares the tool; the clamp ridge grips tapered chisels; screws (5) adjust the movable side</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Settings at a Glance</h3>
      <table class="settings-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Edge angle</th><th>Projection (P)</th><th>Support</th><th>Stone</th><th>Honing</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Bench / paring chisel, softwood, fine work</td><td>20&ndash;25&deg;</td><td>50&ndash;75 mm (2&ndash;3&quot;)</td><td>Vertical, toward the edge</td><td>220 to shape, 1000 to finish</td><td>In jig, horizontal base on leather side, WM-200 at 220 mm, same angle</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bench chisel, general purpose</td><td>25&deg;</td><td>50&ndash;75 mm</td><td>Vertical</td><td>220 / 1000</td><td>As above</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bench chisel for hardwood or mallet work; mortise chisel</td><td>30&deg; (mortise up to 35&deg;)</td><td>50&ndash;75 mm</td><td>Vertical</td><td>220 / 1000</td><td>As above</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Japanese chisel</td><td>25&ndash;30&deg;</td><td>50&ndash;75 mm (shorter if the blade is short)</td><td>Vertical</td><td>1000 as much as possible</td><td>As above; back honed free-hand, minimal</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bench plane iron (bevel-down)</td><td>25&deg;</td><td>50&ndash;75 mm; longer helps camber</td><td>Vertical, use both safety stops</td><td>220 / 1000</td><td>As above</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Block / bevel-up plane iron</td><td>25&deg; standard; 35&ndash;38&deg; for tear-out-prone grain (the bevel sets the cutting angle on these planes)</td><td>50&ndash;75 mm</td><td>Vertical</td><td>220 / 1000</td><td>As above</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Spokeshave blade, cabinet scraper</td><td colspan="5">Too short for the SE-77 shoulder; Tormek recommends the SVD-110 Tool Rest (TNT-808 page) for these</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p08_03.jpg" alt="Diagram of 20, 25 and 30 degree edge angles on a chisel bevel" />
        <figcaption>Edge angle: 25&deg; is the norm for chisels, plane irons and spokeshave blades; 20&deg; for delicate work in soft wood; 30&deg; for hard wood and mallet work</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Preparation (All Tools)</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Check the stone is true.</strong> Lower the universal support until it just clears the stone and rotate the stone by hand. If the gap varies, true it with the TT-50. A wide plane iron will show every error in the stone as a wave in the edge.</li>
        <li><strong>Flatten the back once.</strong> A sharp edge is two polished surfaces meeting; a perfect bevel on a scratched back is still a dull edge. New tools usually have grinding marks on the back. Flatten the last 25&ndash;30 mm (1&ndash;1-1/4&quot;) on the <em>side</em> of the grindstone, holding the tool flat and moving it slightly, with the universal support placed close to the wheel as a rest for the side of the tool. Bring the <strong>heel down first and the edge last</strong>; if the edge touches first it will cut into the stone and round over. Then polish the back on the leather wheel, holding it tangent to the wheel. This is a one-time job per tool.</li>
        <li>Grade the stone coarse if you are changing the shape, or fine if you are only refreshing an edge.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p03_01.jpg" alt="Drawing of a chisel back being flattened on the side of the SG-250 grindstone, resting on the universal support" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p03_02.jpg" alt="Drawing of the back of a chisel being polished free-hand on the leather honing wheel, held tangent to the wheel" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p02_03.jpg" alt="Two drawings: checking the stone is true by letting the universal support nearly touch it, and truing it with the TT-50" />
      </div>

      <h3>Mounting the Tool</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Open the clamp a little wider than the tool is thick, keeping the clamp roughly parallel to the base.</li>
        <li>Slide the tool in with its flat (back) side up against the upper base and its edge pointing toward the stone. Tools with parallel sides go <strong>against the shoulder</strong>, which squares them automatically.</li>
        <li>Set the projection (P) to about <strong>50&ndash;75 mm (2&ndash;3&quot;)</strong> from the jig to the edge. Projection does not affect the angle on the SE-77 (the Micro Adjust does that), but a longer projection makes a plane iron more flexible for cambering and a shorter one is stiffer and more stable for narrow chisels.</li>
        <li>Tighten the knob nearest the tool first, then the other. Position the movable knob about 5 mm from the tool. Firm is enough; the clamp design spreads the force.</li>
        <li>Narrow chisels: centre them under the jig&rsquo;s centre line (marked on the base) so both adjustment screws see the same load.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p03_03.jpg" alt="Three drawings of mounting a chisel in the SE-77: the tool resting against the shoulder, the projection P of 50 to 75 mm, and the clamp knobs being tightened" />
        <figcaption>Mounting: flat side against the shoulder, projection 50&ndash;75 mm, movable knob about 5 mm from the tool</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Safety Stops</h3>
      <p>Two stops keep the jig from sliding off the end of the stone while you traverse. The <strong>outer stop</strong> (B) is a collar clamped on the end of the universal support and is independent of the tool width. The <strong>inner stop</strong> (A) is a movable collar on the bar set so that the tool still has about 6 mm (1/4&quot;) of its edge on the stone at the end of its travel. Use both when sharpening plane irons, which are wider than they look relative to the 50 mm stone and are deliberately run partly off each side. Remove the inner stop for blades wider than 60 mm (2-3/8&quot;). The stops are for the grinding wheel only; when honing, take them off and watch your travel by eye.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p04_01.jpg" alt="Three drawings: the inner stop A set so the tool keeps 6 mm on the wheel, the outer stop B fixed at the end of the universal support, and the plane iron being moved continuously between the stops" />
        <figcaption>Inner stop (A) leaves about 6 mm of edge on the wheel; outer stop (B) is fixed; move the tool continuously between them</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Grinding Technique (All Tools)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Stand so the stone runs toward the edge (universal support vertical). Water will flow over the bevel; that is correct.</li>
        <li>Place the jig on the bar, bring the bevel to the stone and start traversing immediately. <strong>Never let the tool sit still on the stone</strong>; it will groove the stone and leave a hollow in the edge.</li>
        <li>Use the full width of the stone, going no more than about 2 mm (5/64&quot;) past the edge of the stone on either side. Press with your fingers close to the edge; that is where the control is.</li>
        <li>Pressure sets the speed. Press firmly to remove steel, then ease off for the last passes to leave a finer surface.</li>
        <li>Grind until a burr has formed along the <em>entire</em> edge. Feel for it on the back with a fingertip or thumbnail drawn off the edge (never along it). No burr at one corner means that corner has not yet been reached.</li>
        <li>Check the shape often. You control where steel comes off with pressure and dwell time; the jig only controls the angle.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p05_01.jpg" alt="Three drawings: the SE-77 traversing the full width of the wheel, fingers pressing close to the edge, and a fingertip feeling for the burr on the back of the chisel" />
        <figcaption>Traverse the full width, press close to the edge, grind until a burr runs the whole length</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Finishing on the Graded Stone, Then Honing (All Tools)</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Leave the tool in the jig and the universal support exactly as set. Lift the jig off, press the fine side of the SP-650 onto the stone for about 45 seconds with real pressure.</li>
        <li>Put the jig back and grind for 30&ndash;40 seconds with <em>light</em> pressure, still traversing. The coarse scratches disappear and the burr becomes thin and even.</li>
        <li>Move the universal support to the horizontal base on the honing-wheel side. Turn the machine so the leather wheel runs away from you (and away from the edge).</li>
        <li>Set the WM-200 to <strong>220 mm</strong> and to the same angle, or use the marker method on the freshly ground bevel, and lock the bar.</li>
        <li>Hone the bevel with the tool in the jig, traversing gently. Keep part of the edge on the leather at all times; there are no safety stops here.</li>
        <li>Take the tool out and hone the back free-hand, holding it <strong>tangent</strong> to the wheel. Do not tip it steeper than the tangent or you will round the edge over.</li>
        <li>Alternate bevel and back once or twice until the burr is gone. Test: the edge should shave end grain of pine cleanly or slice a sheet of paper from any point without snagging.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p07_02.jpg" alt="Two drawings: the fine side of the stone grader pressed on the stone, and the plane iron in the SE-77 being fine-ground on the graded stone" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p07_03.jpg" alt="Drawing of a plane iron in the SE-77 being honed on the leather wheel with the universal support in the horizontal base" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p08_01.jpg" alt="Two drawings: honing the back of a chisel free-hand tangent to the leather wheel, and a crossed-out example of tipping the handle up steeper than the tangent" />
      </div>

      <h3>Micro-bevels</h3>
      <p>Tormek argues against a secondary bevel. The usual reason for one is that honing a small bevel by hand is faster than honing the whole thing; on the T-8 the whole bevel is honed in the jig in seconds, so that reason evaporates. A single flat bevel also gives a chisel its full bevel length to ride on when paring, which a micro-bevel shortens. If you prefer a micro-bevel anyway (some people like it on plane irons), grind at 25&deg;, then raise the Micro Adjust one or two graduations and hone, or grade fine and take a few light passes, at the steeper angle. The marker method will show you exactly what you have.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p08_02.jpg" alt="Two diagrams comparing the long bevel support of a monobevel chisel with the reduced support length of a chisel with a secondary bevel" />
        <figcaption>Support length with a single bevel (left) versus a secondary bevel (right)</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>(a) Bench Chisels</h3>
      <p><strong>What and why.</strong> Bevel-edge bench chisels from 3 mm to 50 mm, used for paring, chopping and cleaning joints. Western chisels have parallel sides and a flat back, which is exactly what the SE-77 shoulder expects. Target 25&deg; as the all-round angle, 20&deg; for a paring chisel used only by hand in soft wood, 30&deg; for chisels that live under a mallet or in hard woods. The angle is a trade-off between how easily it enters the wood and how long the edge survives.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Assess.</strong> Look at the edge against the light: a bright line means dull, a visible chip means re-shaping. Measure the existing angle in the WM-200 grooves if you want to keep it.</li>
        <li><strong>Prepare.</strong> True the stone if needed. Flatten and polish the back if the tool is new. Grade coarse for a chip or a new angle; grade fine for a touch-up.</li>
        <li><strong>Mount.</strong> Flat side against the shoulder, projection 50&ndash;75 mm, nearest knob first. Narrow chisels under the centre line. Set the inner safety stop so 6 mm of the edge stays on the stone; fit the outer stop.</li>
        <li><strong>Set the angle.</strong> Universal support vertical. New angle: WM-200 set to the stone diameter, angle setter at 25&deg; (or your choice), Micro Adjust until the setter lies flat on the bevel. Existing angle: marker method. Lock both knobs with the nut seated.</li>
        <li><strong>Grind.</strong> Traverse continuously; medium pressure on the fingers near the edge. With a chip, keep going until the chip is gone <em>and</em> a burr runs the full width. For a wide chisel spend a little extra time at the corners; equal pressure everywhere produces a slightly concave edge because the middle spends longer on the stone.</li>
        <li><strong>Check square.</strong> Lift the jig, hold a small square against the side of the chisel and the edge. A long corner is corrected by the adjustable side (see <em>(d)</em>) or simply by spending more time on that side.</li>
        <li><strong>Grade fine and finish</strong> for 30&ndash;40 seconds with light pressure.</li>
        <li><strong>Hone</strong> bevel in the jig at 220 mm / same angle, then back free-hand tangent to the wheel; alternate until the burr is gone. Check the result on end grain.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Typical mistakes:</strong> clamping the chisel on its bevel side (the back must face the upper base); letting the chisel pause on the stone; forgetting to re-set the WM-200 compensator after truing (the angle creeps steeper); honing the back with the handle lifted, which rounds the edge; grinding past the burr until the corners overheat (not possible in water, but you waste steel); and skipping the back-flattening on a new chisel then wondering why it will not get sharp.</p>
      <div class="video-row">
        <div class="video">
