      <h3>(b) Plane Irons, Including Cambered Irons</h3>
      <p><strong>What and why.</strong> Bench plane irons from 35 to 65 mm wide (the jig takes up to 77 mm), block plane irons, and the irons of shoulder, rabbet and bevel-up planes. Standard bevel 25&deg; for bevel-down irons; the cap iron and frog set the cutting angle so the bevel only needs to be durable. On bevel-up planes the bevel angle <em>is</em> the cutting angle, so 25&deg; gives a low-angle shearing cut for end grain and 35&ndash;38&deg; tames tear-out in figured wood. Shoulder and rabbet plane irons must be dead square because their edge meets a shoulder; smoothing, jack and fore plane irons want a <strong>camber</strong>, a gentle curve so the corners of the iron do not leave tracks.</p>
      <p><strong>How much camber.</strong> Tormek&rsquo;s rule of thumb is that the camber (c, the amount the corners are set back from the centre) should roughly match the shaving thickness: about 0.8 mm (1/32&quot;) for a jack plane taking heavy shavings, down to 0.05 mm (0.002&quot;) for a smoother. A jointer sits in between or is left straight. A scrub plane iron needs far more curve than the SE-77 can give; grind it free-hand on the SVD-110.</p>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p06_01.jpg" alt="Diagram of a plane iron with a concave edge, crossed out: the result of equal pressure and even traversing on a wide tool" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p06_02.jpg" alt="Diagram of a cambered plane iron with the camber c marked at both corners" />
      </div>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Prepare.</strong> True the stone; a wide iron reveals any error. Flatten and polish the back of a new iron. Grade coarse for re-shaping, fine for a touch-up.</li>
        <li><strong>Mount.</strong> Back against the upper base, side against the shoulder, projection 50&ndash;75 mm. For a cambered iron use the longer end of that range; the extra leverage lets a thin iron flex a little under finger pressure, which is what creates the camber. A thick, stiff iron (bevel-up planes, premium 3 mm irons) will not flex; there the camber comes from time spent at the corners instead.</li>
        <li><strong>Safety stops.</strong> Set the inner stop so the iron keeps 6 mm on the stone and fit the outer stop. Remove the inner stop for irons wider than 60 mm. The iron is intended to run partly off each side of the 50 mm stone, so the stops matter here more than anywhere.</li>
        <li><strong>Set the angle.</strong> Support vertical. WM-200 at the stone diameter and 25&deg;, or marker method on an existing bevel.</li>
        <li><strong>Grind a straight edge first.</strong> Traverse continuously with even pressure, then <em>add</em> time at the two sides. Equal pressure and even traversing give a concave edge because the centre is on the stone longer than the corners. Check with a straightedge or a small square across the edge until it is straight and a burr runs the full width.</li>
        <li><strong>Grind the camber.</strong> Keep traversing but press harder on the left side as the jig moves left and on the right side as it moves right, shifting your fingertip pressure over the corner that is on the stone. Count passes so both sides get the same treatment. Check the curve by sighting the edge against a square: light should show at both corners and none in the middle. For a smoother a few extra strokes at each corner is all it takes; for a jack plane you will work the corners for a minute or two.</li>
        <li><strong>Larger camber with the adjustable side.</strong> Loosen both adjustment screws (A and B) equally, about a quarter turn at a time. The clamp side can now pendulum, and the pressure you apply at each corner tilts the iron and increases the curve. The more you loosen, the more camber. Centre the iron under the jig&rsquo;s centre line first and make sure it is perpendicular (draw a pencil line on the stone along the universal support and align the edge to it). Return the screws to neutral (the two index lines meet) when you are done.</li>
        <li><strong>Grade fine and finish</strong> with light pressure, repeating the same corner emphasis so the camber survives.</li>
        <li><strong>Hone</strong> in the jig on the leather wheel at 220 mm / 25&deg;, again favouring the corners slightly, then hone the back flat and tangent. A cambered iron is honed exactly like a straight one; the leather conforms.</li>
        <li><strong>Check.</strong> Set the iron in the plane and sight down the sole: the edge should project evenly with the corners just hidden inside the mouth. Take a shaving; it should feather out at both edges with no tracks.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p06_03.jpg" alt="Three drawings: a plane iron centred under the jig's centre line and squared to a pencil line on the stone, the adjustment screws being loosened, and the adjustable side labelled A and B swinging to increase convexity" />
        <figcaption>For a larger camber: centre the iron, square it to a pencil line drawn along the universal support, then loosen screws A and B equally so the side can swing</figcaption>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Typical mistakes:</strong> not using the stops and running the iron off the stone (it drops, the corner digs in and the edge is ruined); even pressure producing a concave edge; cambering before the edge is straight, which bakes the concavity in; forgetting that a bevel-up iron needs a steeper bevel for difficult grain; and setting the honing angle with the compensator still at the stone diameter.</p>
      <div class="video">
        <div class="frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/TIicRhEh57E" title="How to Sharpen a Cambered Plane Iron on Tormek" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
        <p class="caption">Tormek: <em>How to Sharpen a Cambered Plane Iron on Tormek</em> (20:33)</p>
      </div>

      <h3>(c) Mortise Chisels, Thick Tools and Japanese Chisels</h3>
      <p><strong>What and why.</strong> Mortise chisels are thick (often as thick as they are wide), heavy and struck hard, so they want a stout <strong>30&ndash;35&deg;</strong> bevel; 25&deg; crumbles in oak. Firmer chisels, heavy framing chisels and thick bevel-up plane irons behave the same way: a lot of steel to move and a strong edge wanted. Japanese chisels (<em>nomi</em>) are a special case: laminated hard steel on soft iron, a hollow back, short blades with a tapered, non-parallel cross-section, and a 25&ndash;30&deg; bevel.</p>
      <div class="warning">
        <strong>Thickness limit 9 mm (3/8&quot;).</strong> Sash mortise chisels and most modern mortise chisels fit. Big English &ldquo;pigsticker&rdquo; mortise chisels and some framing chisels are thicker than 9 mm and will not go in the clamp; grind those free-hand on the SVD-110 or on the universal support used as a rest, edge-trailing.
      </div>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Mounting a tapered-section tool.</strong> Mortise chisels and Japanese chisels have sides that are not parallel, so the alignment shoulder cannot square them. Instead, with the universal support vertical, lay a pencil along the bar and draw a line on the stone. Mount the tool off the shoulder, under the centre line, and sight the edge against the pencil line while tightening. The ridge in the lower clamp is what lets a short, tapered shank clamp firmly; seat the ridge on the tool before tightening.</li>
        <li><strong>Projection.</strong> 50&ndash;75 mm as usual; on a short Japanese blade use as little as will clear the jig. Thick tools need no extra projection; stiffness is not an issue.</li>
        <li><strong>Angle.</strong> Support vertical. WM-200 at the stone diameter and 30&deg; (mortise) or the existing angle via marker. Lock with the nut seated.</li>
        <li><strong>Stone.</strong> Coarse. A thick bevel is a large area of steel and the coarse surface is the only way to move it in reasonable time. Press firmly, traverse continuously, and reactivate the stone with the coarse side of the SP-650 if it starts to glaze and slow down; thick tools glaze a stone faster than anything else in the kit.</li>
        <li><strong>Grind</strong> until a burr runs the full width. On a narrow mortise chisel the burr forms quickly at the edge but the bevel is deep, so check with the marker that you are reaching the heel as well; otherwise you are steepening the angle a little each time.</li>
        <li><strong>Grade fine and finish</strong> as usual, then <strong>hone</strong> in the jig at 220 mm and free-hand on the back. A mortise chisel does not need a mirror polish on the bevel, but deburring matters; a burr left on a struck chisel folds over and the edge chips.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Japanese chisels in particular.</strong> Be stingy. The hard steel layer is thin and the blade is short, so set the angle carefully with the marker and remove as little as possible; go to the fine-graded stone early. The hollow on the back creeps toward the edge with each sharpening; when it is close, flatten the back on the side of the stone (heel first, edge last, as always) until a narrow flat land is restored behind the edge, then polish on the leather. Hone the back only a few strokes and perfectly flat; any rounding destroys the registration the hollow provides.</p>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p05_02.jpg" alt="Drawing of a Japanese chisel showing its short blade and tapered shank" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p05_03.jpg" alt="Three drawings: drawing a pencil line on the stone along the universal support, aligning a Japanese chisel to that line in the SE-77, and flattening its hollow back on the side of the grindstone" />
      </div>
      <p><strong>Typical mistakes:</strong> trusting the shoulder on a tapered tool (the edge comes out skewed); trying to grind a 30&deg; mortise bevel on a fine-graded stone (slow, and it glazes); forgetting the stone diameter after the heavy wear that thick tools cause; and over-honing the back of a Japanese chisel.</p>

      <h3>(d) Skewed Chisels and Correcting an Out-of-Square Edge</h3>
      <p><strong>Out-of-square.</strong> The common case: a chisel or plane iron comes off the stone with one corner longer than the other, either because the tool arrived that way or because you pressed unevenly. Small errors (a degree or so) are corrected with the SE-77&rsquo;s adjustable side rather than by re-clamping:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Check the edge with a square against the side of the tool and note which corner is long.</li>
        <li>The two small screws (A, upper and B, lower) on the adjustable side act against each other. In the neutral position the two index lines on the side meet. To put <strong>more grinding pressure on the right side</strong> of the tool, loosen A and tighten B; for more on the left, do the opposite. Work in <strong>quarter-turn</strong> steps.</li>
        <li>Grind a few passes, check with the square, adjust again if needed. The tilt is small (it shifts pressure rather than visibly angling the tool) so it is forgiving.</li>
        <li>Once square, continue the normal sequence. Return the screws to neutral before the next tool, or leave them if the same tool tends to come out the same way; a jig can be left dialled in for a particular plane iron.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/se77_p07_01.jpg" alt="Three drawings of the SE-77 fine adjustment: the two adjustment screws creating a movable side, the two index lines meeting in the neutral position, and screws A and B being turned to increase pressure on the right or left side" />
        <figcaption>Fine adjustment: lines meeting = neutral; loosen A and tighten B for more pressure on the right side, about a quarter turn at a time</figcaption>
      </div>
      <p>Larger errors (several degrees) are faster to fix by brute force: grind on the coarse stone with your fingers pressing only on the long corner until it comes back to square, then proceed as normal.</p>
      <p><strong>Deliberately skewed chisels.</strong> Skew paring chisels and the irons of skewed rabbet and block planes have the edge ground at a fixed angle, typically 20&ndash;30&deg;, to the side of the blade. The SE-77 shoulder would square them, so do not use it:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Draw the reference line on the stone along the universal support with a pencil as for a Japanese chisel.</li>
        <li>Mount the tool <em>away from the shoulder</em>, centred under the centre line, and rotate it in the clamp until the existing skewed edge is parallel to the pencil line (or, for a new skew, until a sliding bevel set to the skew angle and held against the side of the blade lines up with the pencil line). Tighten the near knob first and re-check; the tool will try to swing as you clamp.</li>
        <li>Set the angle with the marker method; the WM-200 works too but the angle setter must sit square across the bevel, not along the blade.</li>
        <li>Grind, grade, finish and hone exactly as for a bench chisel. Pairs of skew chisels (left and right) are done one at a time; keep the same projection and jig setting and only mirror the rotation.</li>
        <li>Use the adjustable side to fine-tune the skew angle in quarter turns if the first pass shows it drifting.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Typical mistakes:</strong> turning the adjustment screws both the same direction (that changes camber, not squareness); forgetting to return them to neutral and then wondering why the next chisel comes out skewed; and clamping a skew against the shoulder, which grinds the skew out of it. Turning skews (woodturning) are not SE-77 tools; they go in the SVS-50 Multi Jig on the <a href="/workshop/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/">TNT-808 page</a>.</p>

