      <h2>Honing Wheels: LA-220 Leather and CW-220 Composite</h2>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/la145_p01_01.jpg" alt="Two drawings from the leather honing wheel instruction: the back of the wheel with its two drive pins arrowed, and the wheel being offered up to the machine" />
        <figcaption>Mounting a leather honing wheel: the drive pins on the back of the wheel locate in the holes of the drive wheel</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>Which Wheel</h3>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Wheel</th><th>Size</th><th>Fits</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>LA-220 Leather Honing Wheel</strong> (supplied)</td><td>220 &times; 31 mm</td><td>T-8, T-7 and older 220 mm machines</td><td>Vegetable-tanned cowhide. Needs oil once and PA-70 compound regularly. The classic strop finish: removes the burr gently and polishes the bevel to a mirror.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>CW-220 Composite Honing Wheel</strong> (bought separately)</td><td>220 &times; 34 mm</td><td>T-8, T-7 and older 215&ndash;220 mm machines</td><td>Composite material with the polish built in. No compound, no oil, no black smears on hands or tools; deburrs faster than leather. Can be dampened with a wet sponge for a softer feel. Choose it for a clean environment (kitchen knives, a shared shop) or when you want quick deburring more than a mirror polish.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>LA-120 Profiled Leather Honing Wheel</td><td>120 mm, profiled</td><td>Mounts outside the LA-220 on the T-8</td><td>For the inside (flute) of gouges and V-tools. Part of the TNT-808 kit; see that page.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <h3>Preparing the Leather Wheel (LA-220)</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Mount.</strong> Slide the wheel onto the shaft so the two drive pins on its back drop into the holes in the drive wheel, and tighten the locking knob by hand. Reverse to remove it when a long knife, an axe or a scythe needs the clearance.</li>
        <li><strong>Oil the leather once.</strong> New leather is dry and will not hold compound. Squeeze about <strong>two thirds of the supplied tube</strong> over the running surface of a 220 mm wheel and work it in with your fingers, turning the wheel by hand. The oil is 100% pharmaceutical white oil; a light mineral machine oil does the same job. Let it soak in for a few minutes.</li>
        <li><strong>Apply PA-70 compound.</strong> With the machine <em>off</em>, lay a thin bead of compound around the circumference, turning the wheel by hand.</li>
        <li><strong>Spread it.</strong> Start the machine and press the flat of a tool (the back of an old chisel is ideal) gently against the wheel to spread the compound evenly and press it into the leather.</li>
        <li><strong>Maintain.</strong> One application of compound lasts about 5&ndash;10 tools. When honing slows down or the surface looks dry, add a few drops of oil and a fresh thin bead of compound. Do not let the compound dry out and cake; a little oil keeps it working. Do not re-oil heavily; a soaked wheel smears compound without cutting.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-t8/la145_p01_02.jpg" alt="Three drawings: oiling the leather of the honing wheel from the supplied tube, applying a thin string of honing compound, and spreading the compound by pressing a tool against the running wheel" />
        <figcaption>Oil the leather (about 2/3 of the tube for the 220 mm wheel), apply a thin string of PA-70, spread it with a tool</figcaption>
      </div>
      <p><strong>PA-70 Honing Compound</strong> is a paste of fine abrasive (around 3 &micro;m) in a wax binder. It is what turns the leather from a strop into a very fine polishing wheel. It is also what blackens your fingers; the black is steel, not dirt.</p>

      <h3>Honing Direction</h3>
      <div class="danger">
        <strong>Always hone with the wheel running away from the edge (edge trailing).</strong> Honing into the edge, even once, lets the edge slice into the leather and ruins the wheel. Stand so the leather wheel is nearest you and turning away from you at the top; the edge of the tool points the same way the wheel surface is moving.
      </div>
      <h3>Honing and Deburring</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Why.</strong> Grinding leaves a burr: a wire of steel bent over to the back of the edge. It must be removed gently, not snapped off, or it takes a ragged piece of the edge with it. The leather folds it back and forth and polishes it away. Honing also smooths both faces, which makes the edge sharper, slicker in the cut and longer-lasting.</li>
        <li><strong>In the jig.</strong> For chisels, plane irons, knives and gouges, leave the tool in its jig after grinding, move the universal support to the horizontal base on the honing side, set the WM-200 to 220 mm and the grinding angle (or use the marker), and hone the bevel with light pressure, traversing. The jig keeps the honing angle identical to the grinding angle, which is impossible to guarantee free-hand on a curved gouge.</li>
        <li><strong>Free-hand.</strong> Hold the tool so the face being honed is tangent to the wheel. For the back of a chisel or plane iron this means dead flat on the wheel surface, handle low. Never lift the handle above the tangent; that rounds the edge in a couple of passes. Long knives are held diagonally so the tip clears the grindstone on the other end of the shaft.</li>
        <li><strong>Sequence.</strong> Bevel, back, bevel, back. Two or three alternations with light pressure. Feel for the burr between each; when neither side has one, stop. More honing after that only rounds the edge.</li>
        <li><strong>Test.</strong> Pare end grain of pine, or slice newsprint held in the air from the heel of the edge to the tip. Any catch or tear means a remnant of burr or a dull spot; look at the edge under a strong light for a reflecting line.</li>
        <li><strong>Polishing.</strong> The same wheel polishes brass, copper, silver, aluminium and chrome. Keep a second, cheap wheel if you do a lot of this; non-ferrous metal loads the leather.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>When to Use the Composite Wheel Instead</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You want to deburr quickly without a polish step: the CW-220 cuts faster than leather and compound.</li>
        <li>Clean work: no compound on hands, handles or kitchen knives; nothing to dry out or re-apply.</li>
        <li>Shared or teaching shop: no preparation step for a new user to get wrong.</li>
        <li>If it feels aggressive on fine edges, hold a wet sponge against the running wheel for a moment to dampen it; the honing becomes softer.</li>
        <li>Keep the LA-220 for the mirror finish on plane irons and carving tools, and for when you want the gentlest possible deburring of a very thin edge.</li>
      </ul>

