      <h2>Storage &amp; Cleaning</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>After a session, lower the trough, pour it out, pull the magnetic scraper and wipe the sludge into the bin. Rinse the trough. Do not put the residue down a drain.</li>
        <li>Wipe the housing, universal support and jigs dry. A film of water on the bar will leave rust spots on the jigs&rsquo; bushings over time; a wipe with an oily rag on the bar every few sessions keeps the jigs sliding freely.</li>
        <li>Let the stone air-dry on the machine. Do not store it standing in water and never let a wet stone freeze.</li>
        <li>Cover the machine (Tormek&rsquo;s MH-380 cotton cover comes with the TNT-808 kit) if the shop is dusty. Fine wood dust that reaches the drive wheel makes the friction drive slip.</li>
        <li>Store the WM-200 on its magnet plate on the housing, and the stone grader and truing tool somewhere they will not chip the stone if knocked.</li>
        <li>Keep the honing wheel away from water spray; wet leather sheds compound. If it does get wet, let it dry and re-charge it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Maintenance</h2>
      <div class="warning">
        Unplug the machine before removing wheels or doing anything behind the honing wheel.
      </div>
      <h3>Drive Wheel and Motor Shaft</h3>
      <p>The friction drive is self-adjusting and has no wear parts to replace in normal use, but it depends on a clean, dry contact between the motor shaft and the rubber ring. If the motor runs but the stone slows or stops under load: remove the honing wheel, clean the motor shaft with a degreasing solvent, and freshen the rubber surface by holding a strip of emery cloth or sandpaper against the ring while it runs, starting with light pressure so it does not stall and increasing until the rubber grips again. Test by stopping the stone with your hand: the motor shaft should stop too. Keep water, oil and dust away from this area.</p>
      <h3>Main Shaft and Bearings</h3>
      <p>The stainless main shaft runs in two nylon bearings that are designed with 0.1&ndash;0.2 mm (0.004&ndash;0.008&quot;) of radial play. This does not affect grinding because the load is always downward. They are greased at the factory; re-grease about <strong>once a year</strong>: remove the stone (turn it clockwise by hand and the EzyLock spins off), pull the shaft out with the honing wheel still attached, wipe it, apply any ordinary ball-bearing grease to the shaft and inside the bearings, and reassemble.</p>
      <h3>Changing the Grindstone</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Remove:</strong> turn the stone clockwise by hand; the EzyLock loosens itself. Unscrew it the rest of the way by hand.</li>
        <li><strong>Fit:</strong> slide the stone onto the shaft with the washer behind it, thread the EzyLock on anticlockwise by hand until snug. Grinding tightens it to the right torque automatically. No tools, no hammering.</li>
        <li>Re-true a new stone, re-set the WM-200 compensator and reset the inner safety stop for the new diameter.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Stone Trueness Tolerance</h3>
      <p>The stone should run true radially within &plusmn;0.2 mm (0.4 mm total, 0.016&quot;). Axial (side-to-side) wobble up to &plusmn;0.5 mm is harmless and does not affect grinding. If radial runout is larger than this on a new stone or after a knock, check that the shaft is not bent before truing.</p>
      <h3>Leather Wheel &ldquo;Bump&rdquo;</h3>
      <p>The leather is a strip glued to the rim with a joint that is sanded flush at the factory. After the leather has compressed with use, the glue line can stand slightly proud and you feel a tick once per revolution. Sand the glue line down with abrasive paper wrapped around a flat block, carefully, with the machine off and rotating the wheel by hand.</p>
      <h3>Motor Temperature</h3>
      <p>The single-phase motor runs hot even at idle; a case temperature around 70&deg;C (158&deg;F), uncomfortable to touch, is normal and well inside its 135&deg;C insulation rating. The T-8 is rated for continuous operation.</p>
      <h3>Universal Support and Jigs</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Keep the bar clean and lightly oiled. Do not let the stone grader touch it.</li>
        <li>Check the Micro Adjust nut turns freely; grit in the ACME thread makes fine adjustment stiff. Rinse and oil the thread.</li>
        <li>The SE-77 nylon bushings are replaceable (item 6 in the parts list) if the jig develops side play on the bar.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Trouble</th><th>Probable Cause</th><th>Remedy</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td rowspan="2">Stone not running true</td><td>Normal wear, or grooved by narrow tools</td><td>True with the TT-50; keep tools moving across the stone</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bent shaft (runout beyond &plusmn;0.2 mm radial on a trued stone)</td><td>Replace the main shaft (part 1041, MSK-250)</td></tr>
          <tr><td rowspan="2">Motor runs but stone does not, or stalls under light pressure</td><td>Water, oil or dust on the drive wheel</td><td>Clean the motor shaft with solvent; freshen the rubber with emery cloth while running</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Dusty shop</td><td>Cover the machine when not in use</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Stone cuts slowly, feels slick, steel polishes instead of grinding</td><td>Glazed surface (common after HSS or large bevels), or stone still graded fine</td><td>Coarse side of the SP-650, high pressure, 20&ndash;30 s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Edge comes out concave on a wide tool</td><td>Even pressure and traverse: the centre spends longer on the stone</td><td>Spend more time at the sides</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Edge comes out of square</td><td>Uneven pressure; tool not against the shoulder; tapered tool squared by the shoulder</td><td>Adjustable side (quarter turns); pencil-line alignment for tapered tools</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Angle creeps steeper with each sharpening</td><td>WM-200 compensator not updated as the stone wears</td><td>Read the label scale and reset the compensator; verify with the marker</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Honing angle does not match grinding angle</td><td>WM-200 left at the stone diameter on the leather wheel</td><td>Set the compensator to 220 mm for honing</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Edge rounded after honing</td><td>Back honed with the handle lifted above the tangent; too many passes</td><td>Hold the back dead flat on the leather; stop when the burr is gone</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tool jumps or vibrates during truing</td><td>Feeding the TT-50 too slowly, or cut too deep</td><td>Feed in about 90 s per crossing; max 0.75 mm</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tick once per revolution on the leather wheel</td><td>Glue joint standing proud</td><td>Sand the joint flush with paper on a flat block</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Motor case very hot</td><td>Normal for a single-phase induction motor (~70&deg;C)</td><td>No action</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Water trough will not stay up</td><td>Screw lift not engaged</td><td>Raise with the lift knob until it latches; check the guide rails are clean</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

